{"assets":[],"contentKind":"statement","contestId":"2062","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":1,"role":"title"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"F. Traveling Salescat"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"time_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"time limit per test"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"2 seconds"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"memory_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"memory limit per test"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"512 megabytes"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"body"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"You are a cat selling fun algorithm problems. Today, you want to recommend your fun algorithm problems to $$$k$$$ cities."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"There are a total of $$$n$$$ cities, each with two parameters $$$a_i$$$ and $$$b_i$$$. Between any two cities $$$i,j$$$ ($$$i\\ne j$$$), there is a bidirectional road with a length of $$$\\max(a_i + b_j , b_i + a_j)$$$. The cost of a path is defined as the total length of roads between every two adjacent cities along the path."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For $$$k=2,3,\\ldots,n$$$, find the minimum cost among all simple paths containing exactly $$$k$$$ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"distinct"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":" cities."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"input_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The first line of input contains a single integer $$$t$$$ ($$$1 \\leq t \\leq 1500$$$) — the number of test cases."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For each test case, the first line contains a single integer $$$n$$$ ($$$2 \\leq n \\leq 3\\cdot 10^3$$$) — the number of cities."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then $$$n$$$ lines follow, the $$$i$$$-th line contains two integers $$$a_i,b_i$$$ ($$$0 \\leq a_i,b_i \\leq 10^9$$$) — the parameters of city $$$i$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"It is guaranteed that the sum of $$$n^2$$$ over all test cases does not exceed $$$9\\cdot 10^6$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"output_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For each test case, print $$$n-1$$$ integers in one line. The $$$i$$$-th integer represents the minimum cost when $$$k=i+1$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"samples"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Example"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample"},"children":[{"attrs":{"role":"sample_input"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"\n3\n3\n0 2\n2 1\n3 3\n5\n2 7\n7 5\n6 3\n1 8\n7 5\n8\n899167687 609615846\n851467150 45726720\n931502759 23784096\n918190644 196992738\n142090421 475722765\n409556751 726971942\n513558832 998277529\n294328304 434714258\n"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample_output"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"\n4 9 \n10 22 34 46 \n770051069 1655330585 2931719265 3918741472 5033924854 6425541981 7934325514 \n"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"note"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Note"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the first test case:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" For $$$k=2$$$, the optimal path is $$$1\\to 2$$$ with a cost of $$$\\max(0+1,2+2)=4$$$."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" For $$$k=3$$$, the optimal path is $$$2\\to 1\\to 3$$$ with a cost of $$$\\max(0+1,2+2)+\\max(0+3,3+2)=4+5=9$$$. "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the second test case:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" For $$$k=2$$$, the optimal path is $$$1\\to 4$$$."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" For $$$k=3$$$, the optimal path is $$$2\\to 3\\to 5$$$."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" For $$$k=4$$$, the optimal path is $$$4\\to 1\\to 3\\to 5$$$."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" For $$$k=5$$$, the optimal path is $$$5\\to 2\\to 3\\to 1\\to 4$$$."}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"document"},"index":"F","problemCode":"2062F","schema":2,"sourceKind":"html","sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/contest/2062/problem/F"}